Figure 1: 
Inertial $m$odes in a uniformly rotating convection zone computed using the 2.5D linear model of Bekki et al. (2022b).
(Left) Synthetic spectrum in the inertial frequency range showing frequency-binned mode growth rates, $\Im[\omega]$, in the m–$\Re[\omega]$ plane, where negative values of $\Im[\omega]$ indicate damped modes.
To emphasize the dominant branches of the least damped modes, a relatively large viscosity $u_{\rm t}=3\times 10^{12}$ cm2 s-1 is adopted.
To better visualize mixed modes between prograde and retrograde inertial modes, the dispersion diagram is extended to negative azimuthal orders ($m<0$ with $\Re[\omega]>0$).
(Right) Schematic flow structures of representative modes at $m=6$.
The modes labeled A–H correspond to:
(A) a prograde columnar mode in which the axial vorticity $\zeta_{z}$ has no latitudinal nodes;
(B,C) similar prograde columnar modes with one and two latitudinal nodes, respectively;
(D) a sectoral Rossby mode in which the radial vorticity $\zeta_{r}$ has neither latitudinal nor radial nodes;
(E) similar to D but with one radial node;
(F) similar to D but with one latitudinal node;
(G,H) modes strongly confined inside the tangent cylinder, in which the axial vorticity $\zeta_{z}$ is symmetric and antisymmetric across the equator, respectively.
Red and blue surfaces indicate isocontours of $\zeta_{z}$ in panels A–C and G–H, and of $\zeta_{r}$ in panels D–F.
The modes corresponding to C and F were not discussed in Bekki et al. (2022b).
